From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: MeshGuard <noreply@meshguard.invalid>
Date: Tue, 17 Nov 2026 00:01:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] grpc: recover panics in handlers on both gRPC servers

MeshGuard security patch 0002 (task P2-T10a, validation item V-26; see
docs/planning/evidence/P2-T10a.md of the MeshGuard repository). Not
reported upstream: decision of the project owner, 2026-11-17.

The local unix-socket gRPC server (which also serves the REST API
through grpc-gateway) and the optional remote gRPC server were created
without a recovery interceptor: a panic in any handler terminated
Headscale. V-26 was one such panic (patch 0001 removes its cause); this
patch makes sure no other handler panic can stop the control plane.

Add a unary and a stream interceptor, outermost on both servers, that
log the panic once with the stack of the panicking goroutine and answer
codes.Internal "internal error: request handler panicked" (the panic
value may carry request data and stays in the server log; the REST
gateway answers HTTP 500 {"code":13,...}). The remote server keeps its
authentication interceptor, now wrapped by the recovery. Nothing changes
when no handler panics.

Tests: hscontrol/grpc_recovery_test.go (both interceptors; a real gRPC
server on a unix socket built like the local and the remote server, with
a panicking handler, a panicking interceptor and a panicking stream:
codes.Internal, HTTP 500 through the REST gateway, the server keeps
serving).

Remove this patch when upstream recovers handler panics on its gRPC
servers.
